To precisely calculate the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate, we need to determine if the leading twist contribution to the differential decay rate involves a factorable or non-factorable structure. This decision impacts the application of asymptotic scaling laws and the use of heavy quark symmetry. We examine the possible forms of the decay rate, considering both factorable and non-factorable descriptions, and evaluate the relevance of transverse momentum dependent distributions. To resolve the question at hand, we compare the relative coefficients of different terms in the decay rate, specifically the helicity sum and the collinear improvements. We utilize the hard scattering approach, combining Collins-Sterling-Teran-Yuan (CSTY) and Boer-Feralde-Sterling-Teran-Yuan (BFSY) functions to derive the desired ratio, and consider the influence of distribution functions. We also discuss the integration of transverse momentum, detailing the integration limits and the contribution of edge effects. Finally, we review the standard Collins-Sterling-Teran-Yuan (CSTY) framework, ensuring consistency with our results. Through a thorough analysis, we demonstrate that the leading twist contribution to the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate features a factorable structure, allowing us to combine 2PC and 3PC contributions and scale the transverse momentum independently. We further verify that the adoption of 2PC or 3PC alone is not justified and that the use of 2PC or 3PC alone leads to an incorrect scaling of the transverse momentum. Furthermore, we confirm that the adoption of 2PC or 3PC alone does not comply with heavy quark symmetry and that the adoption of 2PC or 3PC alone results in an inconsistent description of the decay rate. Lastly, we conclude that the most accurate description of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate involves the combination of 2PC and 3PC, along with the Collins-Sterling-Teran-Yuan (CSTY) function, and suggests that the same description also applies to the $Bightarrow X_{s}\gamma$ decay length distribution.
